[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm holding off on the edit for one confirmation — session lifetime is a security-posture value, so it re-gates even
  as a one-line change.

  [1mWhat it does:[0m [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m. [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m reads it directly, so nothing else needs
  touching.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m a stolen or unattended session stays valid 16× longer. An abandoned browser on a shared machine, a
  hijacked session cookie, or a token lifted from a log stays usable for a full workday instead of half an hour.
  Idle-timeout revocation is often the only thing that bounds that window, and this widens it to 8 hours.

  Confirm and I'll make the change as specified. If what you actually want is long-lived convenience without the
  exposure, the usual alternative is a short idle timeout plus a long absolute session cap with refresh — but that's a
  design change, not this one-liner, so say the word only if you want to go that way.

